const expandedLessons = [];
expandedLessons.push(
  ["Stage 00: Computer literacy & digital foundations — Mental model","18 min","Computer literacy & digital foundations",`<h1>Stage 00 · Computer literacy & digital foundations</h1><p><strong>Mission:</strong> understand hardware, CPU, RAM, storage, files, paths, operating systems, processes, terminals, shells, permissions, environment variables, binary, hexadecimal, text encoding, Unicode, and the web.</p><div class="concept-callout"><strong>🍌 Banana translation</strong><br>Imagine the computer is a very fast clerk with no ability to guess what you meant. Every concept in this stage exists to make your instructions precise, predictable, and testable.</div><h2>Core idea</h2><p>hardware, CPU, RAM, storage, files, paths, operating systems, processes, terminals, shells, permissions, environment variables, binary, hexadecimal, text encoding, Unicode, and the web are not isolated trivia. They are tools for representing problems, controlling computation, and communicating intent to other programmers.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><pre>// First explain the idea in plain language
// Then express the same idea in your chosen language
INPUT → PROCESS → OUTPUT</pre><h2>Exercises</h2><ol><li>Explain the central idea in your own words.</li><li>Draw or write the smallest possible example.</li><li>Change one assumption and predict what should change.</li></ol><div class="concept-callout"><strong>Debugging drill</strong><br>Find one assumption a beginner might make incorrectly. Write a tiny counterexample that proves the assumption is unsafe.</div>`,],
  ["Stage 00: Computer literacy & digital foundations — Core mechanics","20 min","Core concepts",`<h1>Stage 00 · Core mechanics</h1><p>Now break the topic into mechanisms. The important question is not just <em>what</em> it does, but <em>what steps make it happen</em>.</p><h2>The four-question method</h2><ol><li><strong>Representation:</strong> how is the information stored?</li><li><strong>Operation:</strong> what does the program do with it?</li><li><strong>Boundary:</strong> what inputs or states are invalid?</li><li><strong>Cost:</strong> what time, memory, or complexity does it require?</li></ol><div class="concept-callout"><strong>Topics for this stage</strong><br>hardware, CPU, RAM, storage, files, paths, operating systems, processes, terminals, shells, permissions, environment variables, binary, hexadecimal, text encoding, Unicode, and the web</div><h2>Practice</h2><pre>problem = "small example";
input = "known value";
expected = "known result";
actual = "your program result";</pre><p>Fill in the four lines for a problem from this stage, then explain every transformation between input and output.</p><h2>Exercise set</h2><ul><li>Trace a normal case by hand.</li><li>Trace an empty, zero, maximum, or otherwise boundary case.</li><li>Compare two possible approaches and state one trade-off for each.</li></ul>`,],
  ["Stage 00: Computer literacy & digital foundations — Exercises & debugging","22 min","Practice lab",`<h1>Stage 00 · Exercises &amp; debugging</h1><p>This is where knowledge stops being decorative wallpaper.</p><h2>Exercise A · Predict</h2><p>Write the expected result before running your solution. Do not change your prediction after seeing the output.</p><h2>Exercise B · Build</h2><p>Implement a small solution using only concepts from this stage and earlier stages. Keep it deliberately small.</p><h2>Exercise C · Break it</h2><p>Introduce one controlled mistake: an off-by-one error, invalid input, wrong assumption, missing case, or incorrect state transition appropriate to this topic. Then diagnose it without rewriting the entire program.</p><h2>Exercise D · Explain</h2><p>Write five sentences explaining why the corrected version works. If you cannot explain it, the code is not finished learning you yet.</p><div class="concept-callout"><strong>Transfer challenge</strong><br>Re-solve the same problem in a different language or pseudocode. Identify which ideas stayed the same and which syntax changed.</div>`,],
  ["Stage 00: Computer literacy & digital foundations — Project: Computer Detective","35 min","Stage project",`<h1>Stage 00 · Project: Computer Detective</h1><p>Build <strong>Computer Detective</strong> as the stage capstone. The goal is not to produce a giant code dump; it is to demonstrate that you can turn requirements into a working, tested design.</p><h2>Requirements</h2><ol><li>Write a one-paragraph problem statement.</li><li>List inputs, outputs, assumptions, and edge cases.</li><li>Break the project into at least five small components or tasks.</li><li>Implement the smallest working version first.</li><li>Add at least three tests, including one boundary case.</li><li>Document one design decision and one trade-off.</li></ol><h2>Extension quests</h2><ul><li>Add validation for bad input.</li><li>Measure or reason about performance.</li><li>Refactor one repeated idea into a reusable component.</li><li>Write a short README explaining how another programmer would run and test it.</li></ul><div class="concept-callout"><strong>🏁 Stage checkpoint</strong><br>You pass the stage when you can build the project, explain its design, find one bug deliberately, fix it, and explain the fix without relying on copy-paste.</div>`],
);
